  "conventions": {
    "cursor_text": "A cursor text is cursor_prefix and then the cursor bytes as unpadded Base64url. The unused bits of the last character are zero. A client refuses a text that does not encode back to the same text, and a text of 0 bytes or of more than max_cursor_bytes bytes.",
    "errors": "The server answers invalid_command to a page block that it cannot read or serve. Examples are a cursor of an unknown kind or length, a cursor whose kind does not fit the order, and a name filter on GetPartitions. reply_too_large is new in this protocol version. The server answers it in place of a GetClients reply, or of a reply to a read of streams, topics, partitions, consumer groups, users, tokens, options, stats or cluster metadata, whose frame is above the reply size cap. A page reply stays far below that cap.",
    "hex": "Every *_hex field is lowercase hex without separators. request_hex is the command payload without framing, and response_hex is the reply payload.",
    "http_query": "http_query holds the page keys and their text values that a client sends to the HTTP list route. A request with limit or cursor gets one page. A request without both gets the whole list as a bare JSON array, in the order of its order and descending keys and filtered by its name key. The partitions route always answers a page.",
    "identifiers": "stream_id and topic_id are {kind, value}: kind numeric with a u32 value, or kind named with a string value. They are null for a command that has no such field.",
    "items": "items hold the id and the name of each item of the page, in reply order. A partition has no name.",
    "name": "A name is UTF-8 text of 1 to max_name_bytes bytes. Its length on the wire counts bytes, not characters. GetPartitions has no name filter, so its query has no name and its page block has a name length of 0. The server refuses a GetPartitions page block with a name.",
    "next": "A client sends the next of a page as the cursor of the next request, with the same other fields. A page can be empty and still have a next. A walk ends at the first page without a next.",
    "opaque_cursors": "A client that encodes the wire format itself does not read the bytes of a cursor. It keeps the bytes of every opaque_cursors entry and of every opaque_responses next. It sends them as it got them, and the server refuses them with invalid_command. The Rust SDK and the SDKs that wrap it read the bytes, so they refuse these cursors and replies.",
    "refused": "A client refuses every text of refused.cursors, every input of refused.queries before it sends a request, and every body of refused.responses. An input with a name is a query of a list with a name filter.",
    "walk": "Over TCP, QUIC and WebSocket, a list method that returns a whole list walks the pages with order id, ascending and limit max_limit. Over HTTP, it reads the route without limit and cursor, which answers the whole list as a bare JSON array."
